cartridge "DESERT FRONT/A.THOMASON/1987"

' DESERT FRONT
' A ColecoVision game based on "Desert Front" by Alan Thomason, Ahoy!
' November 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, DESERT FRONT ML at $C000,
' started by a two-line BASIC loader). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an
' AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/desert-ml.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a desert horizon of dunes, red mesas, rocks and cacti all
' the way round, a sandbagged twin gun seen from behind, a grey raider with
' no markings, smooth sprites, sound effects.
'
' The game: you are the lone outpost on the desert front. A raider circles
' you. Turn the gun (the horizon turns with it), set the height where your
' shells burst, and fire. Shoot it down before your TIME runs out; every
' bomb it drops on you adds to the DAMAGE; firing heats the gun (TEMP).
' Let any of the three gauges run out and the battle is lost. Each raider
' shot down is worth 100 points plus one point for every step left on the
' three gauges; then they are reset and the next raider, a little faster,
' comes in.
'
' From the original (orig/): the panoramic horizon that the stick turns
' (one column a step; the raider and its bomb shift with it); up and down
' set the shell's burst height and the gun's barrel pose (six poses); the
' button fires when no shell is in the air, and keeps firing while held;
' the shell climbs from the gun in the middle of the view and shrinks, and
' only hits near the end of its climb; the three gauges (TEMP, DAMAGE,
' TIME) of four cells with eight steps each: TEMP starts at 1 step and
' rises a step a shot, falls a step every 25 passes while the gun is idle
' and the button is up, never below 1; DAMAGE starts at 1, TIME starts
' full and loses a step at a steady pace (about 28 seconds a raider); a
' full TEMP or DAMAGE or an empty TIME ends the game. The raider: it flies
' left or right; its turn counter (0-139) rises every step, twice as fast
' while it is in the middle of the view, where reaching 110 makes it turn
' round and drop a bomb; at 135 it turns back if it is at the left or
' right of the view and flying out; a turn shows it head-on for a moment.
' It shies away from your aim: when it is more than a set height below the
' burst height it dives, otherwise it climbs, between a ceiling and a
' floor. Out of view it flies back towards you the short way round. The
' bomb grows as it comes at you and hits the outpost. A kill: 100 points,
' the raider burns and falls, the gauges are counted into the score one
' step at a time (DAMAGE up to full, TIME down to empty, TEMP up to full),
' then reset; each raider flies faster (the C64 cut its step delay by 5,
' from 180).
'
' Liberties: the horizon is 256 columns all the way round (the C64 read
' past its 256-byte table into other data at the far end); the raider and
' its bomb shift a whole column (8 pixels) with the horizon (the C64 moved
' them 6 pixels for an 8-pixel column); speeds are worked out for a human
' player: the horizon turns a column every 3 frames (the C64 turned one a
' pass, about 55 a second), the burst height moves a pixel a frame, the
' shell climbs in 12 to 18 frames (the C64 took about a quarter of a
' second), the bomb takes 96 frames to arrive (the C64's grew in 7 passes,
' about an eighth of a second, too fast to see) and can be shot down for
' 10 points (on the C64 a far shell touching the bomb counted as the
' raider shot down); a bomb adds 3 steps of DAMAGE (the C64 added 1, which
' never mattered before TIME ran out); the raider's speed rises to at
' most 1.9 pixels a frame (from the 11th raider; the C64's delay wrapped round to slow
' after 36); the next raider comes in from the far side of the horizon
' (the C64 started it at column 0); a sight shows where the shell will
' burst and arrows at the horizon's edges point to a raider out of view
' (the C64 had neither: only the barrel's pose); the raider shies from the
' sight only when it is within 20 pixels of it, and otherwise flies at a
' cruising height it picks at random at each turn (the C64's raider
' always climbed unless the aim was far above it, so a gun left aimed at
' the ceiling met it there every time); the turn in the middle of the
' view comes when the counter reaches 110 or 111 (the C64 tested 110 only,
' and the counter, rising by two there, could miss it for ever, so a
' raider that the gun followed never turned or bombed); collisions are box tests
' (the C64 used its sprite collision register); GAME OVER says which gauge
' ran out; the best score is kept. The C64's space-bar pause is left out
' (no keypad).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): left and right turn the gun, up and down
' raise and lower the burst height, either button fires. On the title, up
' and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

